We use standardized benchmarks, we test them on multiple-choice legal exams, or we evaluate them on perplexity, which is literally just a metric of how confidently they predict the exact next word.

26:32.780 --> 26:35.840
We are forcing good-hurt dynamics onto them by default.

26:36.180 --> 26:47.480
According to Flyxion's theorem, by relentlessly optimizing for immediate predictive accuracy on static benchmarks, we are actively incentivizing these models to collapse their belief manifold, aren't we?

26:47.480 --> 26:58.060
We absolutely are. We are explicitly rewarding them for memorizing the localized test distribution at the direct expense of preserving the complex geometry required for true, open-ended navigation.

26:58.280 --> 27:00.800
We are literally paying them to destroy their compass.

27:01.200 --> 27:01.520
Yes.

